Organizations routinely fail to detect coherence deterioration before it becomes a crisis. Governance frameworks, performance systems, and architectural reviews consistently arrive late — not because they are poorly designed, but because the conditions that make them necessary are structurally invisible at the moment they are most needed. This paper presents ATHERA (Adaptive Theory of Hierarchical Entropy Regulation in Organizations), a viabilityconstrained theory of organizational coherence dynamics under adaptive pressure.&nbsp;
<div>
 ATHERA formalizes the dynamic conditions under which organizational coherence accumulates entropy, degrades across strategic and operational timescales, and becomes self-sealing against corrective intervention through delay and latent fragility accumulation. The framework introduces a six-component state vector, a ten-equation dynamic system with explicit delay architecture, a six-regime behavioral classification, and a Rupture Risk Index as a scalar viability heuristic.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 The central theoretical contributions are: (1) a formal distinction between visible organizational state and latent organizational trajectory, explaining why systemic deterioration is structurally unobservable until critical thresholds are crossed; (2) the metastability trap, a mechanism by which delay dynamics simultaneously permit hidden deterioration and suppress the regulatory signals required to address it; (3) a multi-timescale coherence architecture in which strategic and operational coherence interact multiplicatively through a weighted product operator; and (4) an asymmetrical propagation assumption establishing that coherence degradation flows upward through organizational layers faster than recovery flows downward. ATHERA v1 does not predict organizational collapse.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 A<span>THERA v1 formalizes the dynamic conditions under which organizational coherence may deteriorate, stabilize, oscillate, or fragment under adaptive pressure and delayed regulation.</span>
</div>

Background. Artificial Intelligence (AI) is reshaping the way organizations manage occupational health and safety (OHS), enabling predictive risk assessment, real-time monitoring and data-driven decision-making. Despite the rapid growth of empirical studies in this field, a current, management-oriented synthesis is lacking.&nbsp;
<div>
 Objective. To identify and synthesize the empirical evidence on the use of AI in OHS management, focusing on the type of AI technologies deployed, the occupational risks addressed, the impact on OHS outcomes, the level of organizational implementation, and the stage of the Plan-Do-Study-Act (PDSA) improvement cycle in which AI is applied.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 Methods. This protocol is reported in accordance with the Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic Review and Meta-Analysis Protocols (PRISMA-P) statement. The systematic literature review (SLR) will be conducted following PRISMA 2020. A structured search will be performed in Scopus, Web of Science, Business Source Complete (EBSCOhost), and ProQuest. Two reviewers will independently screen and extract data; a third reviewer will resolve discrepancies. Methodological quality will be assessed using the JBI Critical Appraisal Tools. A structured narrative synthesis will be performed following the SWiM (Synthesis Without Meta-analysis) guideline and JBI manual for evidence synthesis.&nbsp;
</div>
